BEGIN;

DELETE FROM questions WHERE topic = 'Embryogenesis & Development' AND subtopic IN ('Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation', 'Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer', 'Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus', 'Gestation & Birth', 'Placental Functions');

-- Q1 [Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub1$Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer$sub1$, $qt1$During gas exchange at the placenta, the oxygen partial pressure in fetal blood is only about 20 mm Hg, yet the fetus still extracts oxygen from maternal blood. A student investigating why proposes that the fetus's hemoglobin (HbF) outcompetes maternal hemoglobin (HbA) for oxygen. What property of HbF accounts for this transfer?$qt1$, $op1$[{"label": "A", "text": "HbF binds oxygen more tightly than HbA because two of its four globin subunits have a different structure that raises its oxygen affinity."}, {"label": "B", "text": "HbF binds oxygen less tightly than HbA because its altered globin subunits lower its oxygen affinity."}, {"label": "C", "text": "HbF and HbA have identical oxygen affinities, but HbF is present at a higher concentration in fetal blood."}, {"label": "D", "text": "HbF binds oxygen more tightly than HbA because all four of its globin subunits differ, giving it twice the oxygen-binding sites."}]$op1$::jsonb, 'A', $ex1$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is A because fetal hemoglobin has a greater affinity for oxygen than adult hemoglobin, and the text attributes this directly to two of its four subunits having a different structure. The A&P 2e text states that "Both fetal and adult hemoglobin have four subunits, but two of the subunits of fetal hemoglobin have a different structure that causes fetal hemoglobin to have a greater affinity for oxygen than does adult hemoglobin." Because the partial pressure of oxygen in fetal blood at the placenta is only about 20 mm Hg, this higher affinity is what lets the fetus pull oxygen from maternal blood despite the modest partial-pressure difference. The transfer is driven by an affinity difference, not by hemoglobin concentration or by a change in the number of subunits or binding sites. (Choice A) Correct: HbF retains four subunits, two of which differ structurally, raising its oxygen affinity above that of HbA. (Choice B) Incorrect: this reverses the relationship; the text says the altered subunits give HbF a greater, not lesser, affinity for oxygen. (Choice C) Incorrect: the text attributes oxygen transfer to the affinity difference between HbF and HbA, not to a difference in hemoglobin concentration. (Choice D) Incorrect: only two of the four subunits differ, and the subunit count is the same as HbA, so HbF does not have extra binding sites. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the structural basis of fetal hemoglobin's elevated oxygen affinity as stated in the text.$ex1$, 'easy', '3B', $cc1$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc1$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a1B$Inverting the direction of the affinity difference (HbF lower instead of higher)$a1B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a1C$Attributing fetal oxygen uptake to hemoglobin concentration rather than affinity$a1C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a1D$Overstating how many subunits differ and inventing extra binding sites$a1D$ FROM q;

-- Q2 [Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer | easy | Skill 1 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub2$Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer$sub2$, $qt2$A physiologist measures gas exchange in a healthy near-term human fetus whose lungs are compressed and filled with amniotic fluid. She predicts that oxygen is loaded into the fetal blood and carbon dioxide is unloaded at which site?$qt2$, $op2$[{"label": "A", "text": "At the fetal lungs, which inflate with amniotic fluid to load oxygen before birth"}, {"label": "B", "text": "At the placenta, the organ that provides gas exchange while the fetal lungs are nonfunctional"}, {"label": "C", "text": "At the fetal liver, where the ductus venosus delivers freshly oxygenated blood"}, {"label": "D", "text": "At the amnion, the membranous sac that directly surrounds and protects the fetus"}]$op2$::jsonb, 'B', $ex2$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is B because gas exchange in the fetus is provided by the placenta rather than by the nonfunctional fetal lungs. During fetal life the lungs are compressed, filled with amniotic fluid, and nonfunctional, so the two pulmonary shunts (foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus) divert blood away from them; oxygen is therefore loaded and carbon dioxide unloaded at the placenta, which the text names as the organ of gas exchange between the pregnant person and fetus. (Choice A) The fetal lungs are explicitly compressed, fluid-filled, and nonfunctional in utero and do not load oxygen from amniotic fluid; they expand and take over gas exchange only after the first breath. (Choice B) Correct: the placenta is the organ of gas exchange, supplying oxygen and removing carbon dioxide while the fetal lungs remain nonfunctional. (Choice C) The fetal liver is not a gas-exchange organ; the ductus venosus actually shunts freshly oxygenated blood past (bypassing) the liver to reach the heart directly. (Choice D) The amnion is the fluid-filled sac that protects the fetus from trauma and rapid temperature changes, not the surface where gases are exchanged. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established fact that fetal gas exchange occurs at the placenta while the fetal lungs remain nonfunctional.$ex2$, 'easy', '3B', $cc2$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc2$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a2A$Treating the fluid-filled fetal lungs as the active gas-exchange organ in utero$a2A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a2C$Substituting the liver (a ductus-venosus-bypassed organ) for the gas-exchange site$a2C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a2D$Substituting the protective amnion for the gas-exchanging placenta$a2D$ FROM q;

-- Q3 [Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub3$Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer$sub3$, $qt3$A physiologist measures the partial pressure of oxygen on both sides of the placental barrier in a near-term fetus and finds it is roughly 35-50 mm Hg in the pooled maternal blood and only about 20 mm Hg in the fetal blood, yet the fetus still becomes well oxygenated. Knowing that fetal hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly than maternal hemoglobin does, the physiologist predicts the direction of net oxygen movement across the barrier.$qt3$, $op3$[{"label": "A", "text": "Net oxygen moves from fetal blood into maternal blood because maternal hemoglobin's higher affinity draws oxygen toward the mother."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Oxygen moves in both directions at equal rates, so there is no net transfer of oxygen between the two bloodstreams."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Net oxygen moves from maternal blood into fetal blood because fetal hemoglobin's higher affinity pulls oxygen across the barrier."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Net oxygen moves from maternal blood into fetal blood because the two bloodstreams mix directly in the placenta and equalize oxygen content."}]$op3$::jsonb, 'C', $ex3$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is C because the greater oxygen affinity of fetal hemoglobin lets it strip oxygen from maternal hemoglobin and hold it, driving net oxygen movement from maternal blood into fetal blood despite only a small partial-pressure gradient. The text notes that the partial pressure of oxygen is only about 20 mm Hg in fetal blood versus 35-50 mm Hg in maternal placental blood, a difference small enough that "there is not as much diffusion of oxygen into the fetal blood supply," and that the fetus "overcomes this problem by having a greater affinity for oxygen than maternal hemoglobin." Because affinity, not just the pressure gradient, governs net uptake, oxygen is transferred from the maternal side to the fetal side while carbon dioxide and other wastes pass in the opposite direction. (Choice A) is reversed: it is fetal, not maternal, hemoglobin that has the higher affinity, so oxygen is pulled toward the fetus rather than the mother. (Choice B) is wrong because the affinity difference produces a real net gain of oxygen by the fetus rather than a wash. (Choice C) is correct because fetal hemoglobin's greater affinity drives net oxygen transfer from maternal blood into fetal blood. (Choice D) names the right direction but the wrong mechanism: the fetal and maternal bloodstreams never mix, as they are separated by the chorion, so transfer occurs by diffusion and affinity, not by direct mixing. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires inferring the direction of net oxygen transfer by combining the measured partial-pressure data with the affinity difference between the two hemoglobins rather than recalling a stated fact.$ex3$, 'medium', '3B', $cc3$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc3$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a3A$Swaps which hemoglobin has the higher affinity, reversing the direction of net oxygen flow toward the mother$a3A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a3B$Treats equal bidirectional diffusion as producing no net transfer, ignoring the affinity-driven net uptake by the fetus$a3B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a3D$Correct direction but attributes it to direct mixing of the two bloodstreams, which are actually separated by the chorion$a3D$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub4$Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer$sub4$, $qt4$A fetal physiologist injects a tracer into the single umbilical vein of a near-term fetus and follows the bolus as it enters the ductus venosus. She wants to identify which fetal organ this shunt allows the freshly oxygenated placental blood to skip on its way to the heart. Through or around which organ does the ductus venosus route this blood?$qt4$, $op4$[{"label": "A", "text": "It routes the blood around the fetal lungs, because the lungs are compressed and nonfunctional and the shunt diverts blood from the pulmonary circuit to the heart"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It routes the blood around the fetal spleen, because the spleen would otherwise sequester the incoming oxygenated red blood cells before they reach the heart"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It routes the blood around the fetal kidneys, because the kidneys would otherwise filter out the oxygen and nutrients before the blood reaches the heart"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It routes the blood around the fetal liver, because the shunt lets much of the freshly oxygenated placental blood bypass the liver and go directly to the heart"}]$op4$::jsonb, 'D', $ex4$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is D because the ductus venosus branches from the umbilical vein and shunts much of the freshly oxygenated placental blood past the fetal liver so it travels directly to the fetal heart. OpenStax A&P 2e states that the ductus venosus is a temporary vessel that branches from the umbilical vein, \"allowing much of the freshly oxygenated blood from the placenta...to bypass the fetal liver and go directly to the fetal heart\" (L41363-41365). The shunt that diverts blood around the nonfunctional fetal lungs is a different pair of shunts (the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus), which connect the pulmonary to the systemic circuit, not the ductus venosus (L41345-41362). The spleen and kidneys are not bypassed by any of the three named fetal shunts. (Choice A) is incorrect because bypassing the lungs is accomplished by the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus, the two shunts that divert blood from the pulmonary to the systemic circuit, not by the ductus venosus (L41345-41362). (Choice B) is incorrect because no fetal shunt is described as bypassing the spleen, and the spleen does not sequence the umbilical inflow in this way (L41363-41365). (Choice C) is incorrect because the kidneys are not among the organs bypassed by the three fetal shunts, and the ductus venosus specifically targets the liver (L41363-41365). (Choice D) is correct because the ductus venosus branches from the umbilical vein and lets the freshly oxygenated placental blood bypass the fetal liver to reach the heart directly (L41363-41365). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires tracing a labeled bolus through a specific named shunt and inferring which organ that shunt is built to bypass, distinguishing it from the two lung-bypassing shunts rather than recalling a single fact.$ex4$, 'hard', '3B', $cc4$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc4$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a4A$States a true fact about a different pair of fetal shunts (foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus bypass the lungs) and misattributes it to the ductus venosus$a4A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a4B$Invents a spleen-bypass role for the shunt that no fetal shunt actually performs, built on a plausible-sounding RBC-sequestration rationale$a4B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a4C$Substitutes the kidney for the liver as the bypassed organ, confusing the filtration/excretion step with the organ the ductus venosus actually skips$a4C$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub5$Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer$sub5$, $qt5$At the placenta, oxygen in maternal blood sits at a partial pressure of roughly 35–50 mm Hg while fetal blood arriving at the placenta is at only about 20 mm Hg, leaving a modest pressure gradient to drive oxygen across the chorion. A physiologist asks how the fetus still manages to load adequate oxygen across so shallow a gradient. Which mechanism BEST accounts for this?$qt5$, $op5$[{"label": "A", "text": "Fetal hemoglobin's left-shifted dissociation curve gives it high affinity, so it becomes substantially saturated even at the low ~20 mm Hg present in placental blood and keeps drawing oxygen released by maternal hemoglobin."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fetal placental pO2 (~20 mm Hg) is actually higher than maternal placental pO2 (~35–50 mm Hg), so a steep gradient drives oxygen rapidly into fetal blood despite the small difference in values."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The low pO2 and high CO2 of fetal blood shift the fetal hemoglobin curve to the right via the Bohr effect, making fetal hemoglobin pick up oxygen more readily at the placenta."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fetal erythrocytes carry far more 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate than maternal cells, which raises fetal hemoglobin's oxygen affinity and pulls oxygen across the placenta into fetal blood."}]$op5$::jsonb, 'A', $ex5$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is A because fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has an intrinsically left-shifted (higher-affinity) dissociation curve, so it loads oxygen efficiently even at the low partial pressures found in placental blood. The text states that maternal placental pO2 is "about 35 to 50 mm Hg" while fetal blood at the placenta is "about 20 mm Hg," so "there is not as much diffusion of oxygen into the fetal blood supply," and the fetus "overcomes this problem by having a greater affinity for oxygen than maternal hemoglobin" owing to two structurally distinct subunits. Because of that higher affinity, HbF reaches substantial saturation at the low ~20 mm Hg prevailing in the placenta and keeps accepting oxygen unloaded by maternal hemoglobin even though the maternal-to-fetal gradient is shallow. (Choice A) is correct: a left-shifted, high-affinity HbF curve loads oxygen well at low pO2, compensating for the small gradient. (Choice B) is wrong because fetal placental pO2 (~20 mm Hg) is lower, not higher, than maternal (~35–50 mm Hg), so the gradient is small, not steep. (Choice C) is wrong because a rightward Bohr shift lowers affinity and promotes oxygen dissociation/unloading, the opposite of what efficient loading requires; HbF's advantage comes from its intrinsically left-shifted curve. (Choice D) is wrong because elevated BPG actually reduces hemoglobin's oxygen affinity by promoting dissociation, and the text attributes HbF's higher affinity to its different subunit structure, not to BPG. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires combining the quantitative pO2 data with the mechanism of the oxygen–hemoglobin dissociation curve to predict how oxygen loading succeeds despite a shallow gradient.$ex5$, 'hard', '3B', $cc5$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc5$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a5B$Inverts the maternal/fetal pO2 ordering to invent a steep gradient$a5B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a5C$Confuses a rightward Bohr shift (unloading) with the loading advantage of HbF$a5C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a5D$510-trap citing real BPG factor but reversing its effect on affinity and misattributing HbF's affinity$a5D$ FROM q;

-- Q6 [Placental Functions | easy | Skill 1 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub6$Placental Functions$sub6$, $qt6$A full-term fetus in utero has lungs that are compressed and filled with amniotic fluid. A physiology student is asked to name the structure responsible for delivering oxygen to the fetal blood and removing the carbon dioxide it generates.$qt6$, $op6$[{"label": "A", "text": "The fetal lungs, which extract oxygen directly from inhaled amniotic fluid"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The placenta, which mediates maternal-to-fetal oxygen and fetal-to-maternal carbon dioxide transport"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The placenta, which digests maternal proteins while the fetal kidneys supply oxygen"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The placenta, which filters fetal nitrogenous wastes that simultaneously deliver fetal oxygen"}]$op6$::jsonb, 'B', $ex6$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is B because the placenta is the fetal organ of respiratory gas exchange, serving the role the lungs cannot perform in utero. OpenStax A&P 2e lists "Respiration" as a core placental function: the placenta "Mediates maternal-to-fetal oxygen transport and fetal-to-maternal carbon dioxide transport" (lines 57869-57878). The text also states that during fetal life "the lungs are compressed, filled with amniotic fluid, and nonfunctional, and gas exchange is provided by the placenta" (lines 41347-41348), so the lungs themselves do no gas exchange. Nutrition/digestion and waste excretion are separately listed placental functions distinct from respiration (lines 57862-57882). (Choice A) is wrong because the fetal lungs are nonfunctional and fluid-filled in utero; they do not extract oxygen from amniotic fluid. (Choice B) is correct because respiration—maternal-to-fetal oxygen transport and fetal-to-maternal carbon dioxide transport—is an explicitly listed placental function. (Choice C) is wrong because, although the placenta does mediate amino-acid transfer and nutrient storage, gas exchange is its respiratory function and is not performed by the fetal kidneys. (Choice D) is wrong because filtering fetal nitrogenous wastes into maternal blood is a separate listed function from respiration; waste excretion does not itself deliver oxygen. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling that the placenta, not the immature fetal lungs, is the organ of gas exchange in the fetus.$ex6$, 'easy', '3B', $cc6$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc6$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a6A$belief that the fetal lungs perform gas exchange in utero by using amniotic fluid$a6A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a6C$correct placental nutrition/storage function paired with a false oxygen source (fetal kidneys)$a6C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a6D$conflating the separate placental functions of waste excretion and respiration$a6D$ FROM q;

-- Q7 [Placental Functions | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub7$Placental Functions$sub7$, $qt7$A perinatal physiologist labels three solutes that cross the placenta from maternal blood into the fetal bloodstream: oxygen, glucose, and a pool of amino acids together with iron. She notes that the amino acids and iron must be delivered even when their concentrations are already higher on the fetal side than in maternal blood. By which transport mechanism does the placenta move the amino acids and iron across into the fetal circulation?$qt7$, $op7$[{"label": "A", "text": "Simple diffusion, the same passive route that carries oxygen and carbon dioxide down their gradients"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Facilitated diffusion, the same carrier-mediated but passive route used for water-soluble glucose"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Active transport, an ATP-dependent route able to pump them against their concentration gradients"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Bulk flow of maternal plasma passing directly into the fetal capillaries through the villus wall"}]$op7$::jsonb, 'C', $ex7$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is C because the fetus has a high demand for amino acids and iron, and the placenta moves those substances by active transport, the only listed mechanism that can use ATP to drive solutes against their concentration gradient. The text states, "The fetus has a high demand for amino acids and iron, and those substances are moved across the placenta by active transport," which fits the scenario in which the solutes are already more concentrated on the fetal side and so cannot move there passively. By contrast, the text reserves simple diffusion for "Oxygen, carbon dioxide, and any other lipid-soluble substances" and facilitated diffusion for "water-soluble glucose," neither of which can pump a solute up its gradient. The text also notes that "maternal and fetal bloodstreams never mix directly," so no plasma flows from one circulation into the other. (Choice A) Simple diffusion moves oxygen, carbon dioxide, and lipid-soluble substances down their gradients and cannot push amino acids and iron toward the higher-concentration fetal side. (Choice B) Facilitated diffusion is carrier-mediated but still passive and handles water-soluble glucose, so it too cannot move solutes against a gradient. (Choice C) Active transport is correct because it is ATP-dependent and can pump amino acids and iron against their concentration gradients to satisfy fetal demand. (Choice D) Bulk flow of maternal plasma into fetal capillaries does not occur because maternal and fetal blood never commingle; solutes cross the villus wall by transport, not by plasma flow. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires inferring from the stated against-the-gradient condition that only an energy-requiring mechanism can account for the observed delivery, rather than recalling a single labeled fact.$ex7$, 'medium', '3B', $cc7$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc7$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a7A$Applying the passive simple-diffusion route (oxygen/CO2/lipid-soluble) to solutes that actually require active transport$a7A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a7B$Correctly identifying facilitated diffusion as a real placental mechanism but misassigning it to amino acids/iron instead of glucose$a7B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a7D$Assuming maternal and fetal blood mix so solutes arrive by direct plasma flow rather than membrane transport$a7D$ FROM q;

-- Q8 [Placental Functions | medium | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub8$Placental Functions$sub8$, $qt8$An immunologist notes that a fetus inherits paternal genes and therefore displays surface antigens the pregnant person's immune system would normally treat as foreign, yet the fetus is not rejected like a mismatched tissue graft. Examining the placenta, she finds that although oxygen, glucose, and nutrients are exchanged freely, no blood cells of either circulation cross from one side to the other. Which consequence of this cellular impermeability most directly explains why the fetus escapes immune destruction?$qt8$, $op8$[{"label": "A", "text": "It keeps lipid-soluble fetotoxic agents such as alcohol and nicotine from ever reaching the fetal tissues."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It blocks maternal oxygen and glucose from diffusing into fetal blood, forcing the fetus onto its own metabolism."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It prevents maternal antibodies from ever entering the fetal circulation, leaving the fetus with no inherited immunity."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It keeps the pregnant person's cytotoxic T cells from reaching and destroying the fetus, which bears non-self antigens."}]$op8$::jsonb, 'D', $ex8$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is D because the placenta's barrier to blood cells keeps the pregnant person's cytotoxic T cells away from the fetal tissues, so the immune cells never reach the non-self (paternal) antigens they would otherwise attack. The text states that "Maternal and fetal blood does not commingle because blood cells cannot move across the placenta. This separation prevents the pregnant person's cytotoxic T cells from reaching and subsequently destroying the fetus, which bears 'non-self' antigens." The same impermeability also keeps fetal red blood cells out of the parent's circulation so they cannot trigger antibody development until late pregnancy or birth. Crucially, the barrier is selective: it still permits two-way exchange of oxygen, carbon dioxide, glucose, and nutrients, and it does not stop everything from crossing. (Choice A) is wrong because the placenta is in fact permeable to lipid-soluble fetotoxic substances such as alcohol, nicotine, and barbiturates, which is precisely why pregnant people are warned to avoid them. (Choice B) reverses the actual physiology: the barrier permits oxygen to cross by simple diffusion and glucose by facilitated diffusion, so maternal nutrients do reach fetal blood. (Choice C) is a partial truth because, while blood cells cannot cross, maternal IgG does cross the placenta to confer naturally acquired passive immunity, so the fetus is not left without inherited protection. (Choice D) correctly identifies the immunological rationale for keeping the two bloodstreams separate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting a scenario about graft-like rejection and using the placenta's selective cellular impermeability to predict which specific immune mechanism is interrupted.$ex8$, 'medium', '3B', $cc8$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc8$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a8A$Treats the placental barrier as blocking lipid-soluble toxins, when the text states it is permeable to them$a8A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a8B$Inverts a function the barrier permits (nutrient/gas exchange) into something it blocks$a8B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a8C$Correct that blood cells cannot cross, but ignores that maternal IgG does cross to give passive immunity$a8C$ FROM q;

-- Q9 [Placental Functions | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub9$Placental Functions$sub9$, $qt9$A surgeon must remove an ovary containing the corpus luteum from a patient who is 20 weeks pregnant, well after placentation is complete and the corpus luteum has degenerated. A medical student is asked to predict the effect of this procedure on the pregnancy and to identify the reason.$qt9$, $op9$[{"label": "A", "text": "The pregnancy continues, because the placenta has itself taken over secreting the progesterone and estrogen that maintain the endometrium."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The pregnancy is immediately lost, because the corpus luteum is the only structure capable of secreting the progesterone that maintains the endometrium."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The pregnancy continues, because the fetal adrenal glands take over secreting the progesterone that the lost corpus luteum no longer provides."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The pregnancy continues, because the placenta sharply raises its hCG output to directly maintain the endometrium in place of progesterone."}]$op9$::jsonb, 'A', $ex9$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is A because by 20 weeks the placenta, not the corpus luteum, is the organ that synthesizes and secretes the progesterone and estrogen that maintain the pregnancy. OpenStax A&P 2e states that "the placenta takes over the synthesis and secretion of progesterone throughout pregnancy as the corpus luteum degenerates," and OpenStax Biology 2e confirms that by the second trimester "the placenta has taken over... the production of estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum, which has degenerated." Because the corpus luteum is no longer the steroid source at this stage, removing it does not withdraw the progesterone and estrogen holding the endometrium and pregnancy intact, so the pregnancy continues. (Choice A) is correct because once placentation is complete the placenta itself secretes the maintaining steroids, making the corpus luteum dispensable. (Choice B) is true only in very early pregnancy; the mature placenta secretes its own progesterone and estrogen, so corpus luteum removal at 20 weeks is not fatal to the pregnancy. (Choice C) wrongly assigns the steroid-maintenance role to the fetal adrenal; the fetal and maternal adrenals supply weak androgen precursors that the placenta converts to estrogen, but it is the placenta that secretes the maintaining progesterone and estrogen. (Choice D) reverses the relationship, because hCG promotes progesterone synthesis and earlier sustained the corpus luteum; it is progesterone and estrogen, now from the placenta, that maintain the endometrium. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the outcome of a surgical intervention by integrating the timeline of placental endocrine takeover with the role of the corpus luteum.$ex9$, 'hard', '3B', $cc9$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc9$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
