-- Q10 [Placental Functions | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub10$Placental Functions$sub10$, $qt10$A pediatric immunologist measures antibody levels in umbilical cord blood drawn from a healthy full-term newborn and finds substantial concentrations of one immunoglobulin class that was synthesized by the pregnant person's plasma cells, not the fetus, and that protects the infant during its first months of life. The clinician concludes this protection is a form of naturally acquired passive immunity transferred before birth. Which immunoglobulin class did the placenta transport from maternal blood to the fetus to confer this protection?$qt10$, $op10$[{"label": "A", "text": "IgA, because this antibody reaches the infant and protects it from disease during the first months of life"}, {"label": "B", "text": "IgG, because this antibody is transferred from the maternal circulation across the placenta and protects the newborn for its first months of life"}, {"label": "C", "text": "IgM, because this antibody is the first one made during a primary response and is transferred in large amounts to the fetus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "IgE, because this antibody crosses the placenta and pre-arms fetal mast cells against future allergens"}]$op10$::jsonb, 'B', $ex10$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is B because, of the five immunoglobulin classes, IgG is the one that crosses the placenta from the maternal circulation to the fetus, conferring naturally acquired passive immunity that protects the developing fetus and the newborn for the first few months of its life. The text states that "this class of antibody is the one that crosses the placenta to protect the developing fetus from disease" and that "IgG is transferred from the maternal circulation to the fetus via the placenta, protecting the fetus from infection and protecting the newborn for the first few months of its life." Because this antibody is made by the pregnant person and passed to the fetus without the infant mounting its own response, it represents passive (not active) immunity, and because it occurs naturally during fetal development rather than by injection, it is naturally acquired. The placenta acts as a selective barrier, and IgG is the class whose properties permit this transfer. (Choice A) IgA does reach and protect the newborn, but it is delivered through the mother's breast milk (colostrum) after birth, not transported across the placenta before birth, so it is not the trans-placental class. (Choice B) is correct because IgG is the specific antibody class transferred across the placenta to give the fetus and newborn passive immunity. (Choice C) IgM is indeed the first antibody made during a primary response and is the largest antibody, but its size restricts it largely to the blood and it does not cross the placenta. (Choice D) IgE binds mast cells and mediates allergy and anaphylaxis; it is present in the lowest blood concentration and is not the protective class transported across the placenta. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting the cord-blood scenario, recognizing the transfer as naturally acquired passive immunity, and reasoning from antibody-class properties to identify which single class the placental barrier selectively transports.$ex10$, 'hard', '3B', $cc10$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc10$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
