-- Q11 [Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus | easy | Skill 1 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub11$Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus$sub11$, $qt11$During a fetal echocardiogram, a cardiologist tracks blood crossing the foramen ovale, a shunt that lets blood skip the nonfunctional fetal lungs. Between which two structures does this opening allow blood to pass directly?$qt11$, $op11$[{"label": "A", "text": "From the right ventricle to the left ventricle, through an opening across the interventricular septum"}, {"label": "B", "text": "From the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, through a short muscular vessel joining the two great arteries"}, {"label": "C", "text": "From the right atrium to the left atrium, through an opening in the interatrial septum"}, {"label": "D", "text": "From the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava, through a vessel that bypasses the immature liver"}]$op11$::jsonb, 'C', $ex11$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is C because the foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that shunts blood directly from the right atrium to the left atrium, allowing it to bypass the pulmonary circuit. The text states that "the foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that allows blood to flow from the right atrium to the left atrium," and elsewhere describes it as "a shunt that directly connects the right and left atria and avoids the pulmonary trunk altogether." Because the fetal lungs are compressed and nonfunctional, this atrial-level shunt diverts oxygenated blood into the systemic circuit instead of forcing it through the high-resistance pulmonary vessels. (Choice A) is incorrect because the foramen ovale lies in the interatrial septum, not the interventricular septum; an opening between the ventricles describes a ventricular septal defect, not the normal fetal shunt. (Choice B) is incorrect because the short muscular vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta is the ductus arteriosus, a different shunt. (Choice C) is correct because the foramen ovale connects the right atrium to the left atrium across the interatrial septum. (Choice D) is incorrect because the vessel branching from the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava that bypasses the liver is the ductus venosus, the third fetal shunt. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the defined anatomical connection made by a specific fetal cardiac shunt.$ex11$, 'easy', '3B', $cc11$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc11$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
