-- Q12 [Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus | medium | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub12$Foramen Ovale & Ductus Arteriosus$sub12$, $qt12$In a healthy near-term fetus, the right ventricle ejects blood into the pulmonary trunk, where the ductus arteriosus shunts the majority of that blood away from the high-resistance pulmonary vessels. A physiologist tracks this diverted blood to determine where most of it travels next.$qt12$, $op12$[{"label": "A", "text": "Into the pulmonary arteries, where it fully perfuses and inflates the developing fetal lungs"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Into the left atrium, after crossing the interatrial septum from the right atrium"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Into the inferior vena cava, where it rejoins returning systemic venous blood"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Into the aorta, where it bypasses the nonfunctional lungs and enters the systemic circuit"}]$op12$::jsonb, 'D', $ex12$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is D because the ductus arteriosus is a short, muscular vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, and most of the blood pumped from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk is thereby diverted into the aorta. Because the fetal lungs are compressed and nonfunctional, only enough blood reaches them to maintain the developing lung tissue; the uninflated pulmonary vessels have high resistance, which encourages blood to flow instead toward the lower-resistance aorta. This shunt therefore delivers the diverted blood directly into the systemic circuit, bypassing the pulmonary circuit. (Choice A) is incorrect because only a trickle reaches the lungs to maintain developing lung tissue, not enough to fully perfuse or inflate them. (Choice B) is incorrect because right-to-left atrial flow across the interatrial septum is the path of the foramen ovale, not the ductus arteriosus. (Choice C) is incorrect because the connection to the inferior vena cava belongs to the ductus venosus, which links the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava. (Choice D) is correct because the ductus arteriosus diverts most pulmonary-trunk blood into the aorta and the systemic circuit. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the destination of a specific volume of blood by reasoning from the structural connection of the shunt and the high pulmonary resistance of the fetal lungs.$ex12$, 'medium', '3B', $cc12$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc12$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
