-- Q18 [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub18$Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation$sub18$, $qt18$A developmental anatomist studies a fetus in which the ductus venosus fails to form, while all other vessels and shunts develop normally. Compared with a typical fetus, how is the routing of oxygenated umbilical-vein blood through the liver most likely altered?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "A larger fraction of the oxygenated umbilical-vein blood is forced through the hepatic circulation before reaching the inferior vena cava."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A larger fraction of the oxygenated blood is diverted away from the inferior vena cava and into the pulmonary trunk instead."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Deoxygenated blood from the umbilical arteries backs up into the placenta rather than reaching the liver."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The hepatic circulation loses nearly all of its blood supply, leaving the fetal liver ischemic."}]$op18$::jsonb, 'A', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is A because the ductus venosus is the shunt that normally lets most placental blood skip the liver, so its absence forces that blood through the hepatic circulation instead. In a typical fetus, oxygenated blood from the umbilical vein "flows toward the inferior vena cava, all but bypassing the immature liver, via the ductus venosus shunt," and "the liver receives just a trickle of blood." If that bypass never forms, the umbilical-vein blood has no shortcut to the inferior vena cava and must instead traverse the hepatic vasculature, so liver perfusion rises rather than falls. (Choice A) is correct: removing the bypass redirects more blood through the liver before it reaches the inferior vena cava. (Choice B) is wrong because diverting blood toward or around the pulmonary trunk is the job of the ductus arteriosus, which "connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta" and diverts right-ventricular output away from the lungs; that shunt is unrelated to the ductus venosus. (Choice C) is wrong because the ductus venosus carries oxygenated umbilical-VEIN blood toward the heart, not deoxygenated umbilical-artery blood, since "the two umbilical arteries carry blood low in oxygen" toward the placenta. (Choice D) is wrong because the liver normally receives only "just a trickle of blood," and losing the bypass would give it MORE blood, not render it ischemic. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting a physiological consequence by reasoning from the normal bypass function to what happens when that pathway is removed, rather than recalling a stated fact.$ex18$, 'medium', '3B', $cc18$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
