-- Q19 [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub19$Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation$sub19$, $qt19$A physiologist is asked to explain why, in a healthy human fetus, the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus together route most blood away from the pulmonary circuit rather than through it. Which rationale correctly accounts for this diversion?$qt19$, $op19$[{"label": "A", "text": "The fetal lungs consume so much oxygen that diverting blood away from them prevents the rest of the fetal body from becoming hypoxic."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Gas exchange occurs at the placenta while the uninflated, fluid-filled fetal lungs are nonfunctional and offer high resistance to flow."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The fetal lungs would over-oxygenate the blood, so the shunts dilute it before it reaches the systemic circulation."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Pressure in the left atrium exceeds that in the right, pushing blood through the lungs unless it is shunted away."}]$op19$::jsonb, 'B', $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is B because the fetus performs gas exchange at the placenta, not in the lungs, so blood gains nothing by passing through the pulmonary circuit, and the collapsed, fluid-filled lungs physically resist flow. The text states that during fetal life "the lungs are compressed, filled with amniotic fluid, and nonfunctional, and gas exchange is provided by the placenta," and that "blood vessels of uninflated lungs have high resistance to flow, a condition that encourages blood to flow to the aorta, which presents much lower resistance." The foramen ovale shunts blood from the right atrium to the left, and the ductus arteriosus diverts blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, so only a small volume passes through the immature pulmonary circuit. (Choice A) is wrong because the immature pulmonary circuit has only "minor metabolic requirements" — it is bypassed because it is nonfunctional, not because it is oxygen-greedy. (Choice B) is correct because placental gas exchange plus high pulmonary resistance together explain why blood is routed away from the lungs. (Choice C) is wrong because the fetal lungs cannot oxygenate blood at all; the respiratory system is immature and "cannot yet oxygenate blood on its own," and the placenta does the gas exchange. (Choice D) reverses the actual pressure relationship: in the fetus right-atrial pressure exceeds left-atrial pressure, driving blood through the foramen ovale, and inflation of the lungs at the first breath reverses this so flow temporarily moves left-to-right. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires reasoning from the fact that the placenta is the gas-exchange organ to deduce the functional reason two shunts must bypass the lungs.$ex19$, 'medium', '3B', $cc19$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
