-- Q20 [Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub20$Ductus Venosus & Fetal vs Adult Circulation$sub20$, $qt20$A neonatologist studying fetal circulation notes that the freshly oxygenated blood returning from the placenta enters the fetal body through the single umbilical vein but largely skips the still-immature fetal liver before reaching the heart. The neonatologist wants to identify which vessel carries this oxygen-rich blood and the shunt that allows it to avoid the liver on its way to the heart.$qt20$, $op20$[{"label": "A", "text": "The single umbilical vein carries the oxygen-rich blood, and the ductus venosus routes it through the liver before reaching the inferior vena cava."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The single umbilical vein carries the oxygen-rich blood, and the foramen ovale carries it past the liver to the inferior vena cava."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The two umbilical arteries carry the oxygen-rich blood, and the ductus venosus carries it past the liver to the inferior vena cava."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The single umbilical vein carries the oxygen-rich blood, and the ductus venosus carries it directly past the liver to the inferior vena cava."}]$op20$::jsonb, 'D', $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is D because the single umbilical vein carries the freshly oxygenated blood emerging from the placenta, and the ductus venosus is the shunt that branches from that vein and channels much of the blood directly to the inferior vena cava, bypassing the immature fetal liver. The text states the umbilical vein "carries oxygen-rich blood from the pregnant person to the fetal inferior vena cava via the ductus venosus," and describes the ductus venosus as a vessel that "branches from the umbilical vein, allowing much of the freshly oxygenated blood from the placenta...to bypass the fetal liver and go directly to the fetal heart." A parallel passage confirms that "from the umbilical vein, the oxygenated blood flows toward the inferior vena cava, all but bypassing the immature liver, via the ductus venosus shunt," so the liver receives only a trickle while the bulk of oxygenated blood is shunted onward. (Choice A) names the correct vessel and shunt but reverses the shunt's function, routing blood through the liver when the ductus venosus exists precisely to bypass it. (Choice B) keeps the correct vessel but substitutes the foramen ovale, which is an interatrial opening from the right atrium to the left atrium, not the cord-to-IVC liver-bypass conduit. (Choice C) keeps the correct shunt but names the umbilical arteries, which actually carry oxygen-depleted, waste-laden blood toward the placenta. (Choice D) is correct because the umbilical vein is the oxygenated vessel and the ductus venosus carries that blood directly to the inferior vena cava, skipping the liver. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires linking the identity of the most-oxygenated fetal vessel to the specific shunt and routing that deliver its blood to the heart, while rejecting anatomically real but misrouted alternatives.$ex20$, 'hard', '3B', $cc20$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 155
  ) RETURNING id
)
