UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a20B$Keeps the correct oxygenated vein but substitutes the foramen ovale (an interatrial shunt) for the ductus venosus as the liver-bypass conduit to the IVC.$a20B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a20C$Keeps the correct ductus venosus but applies the adult artery-equals-oxygenated rule, naming the umbilical arteries as the high-oxygen vessel.$a20C$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Gestation & Birth | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub21$Gestation & Birth$sub21$, $qt21$A genetics counselor reviewing a patient's prenatal chart notes that, at exactly 10 weeks of gestation, the developing human is officially designated a fetus rather than an embryo. During which span of gestation is a developing human correctly designated a fetus?$qt21$, $op21$[{"label": "A", "text": "From the ninth week of gestation until birth, after organogenesis has established the rudimentary structures of all organs"}, {"label": "B", "text": "From the moment of fertilization until birth, since the diploid conceptus is a developing human throughout that span"}, {"label": "C", "text": "From the third week of gestation, when gastrulation establishes the three germ layers and the developing human is so named"}, {"label": "D", "text": "From the second trimester onward, once quickening and powerful limb movements begin around weeks 16 to 20"}]$op21$::jsonb, 'A', $ex21$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is A because the textbook defines the fetal period as beginning at the ninth week of gestation and continuing until birth, immediately following the embryonic period. OpenStax states that "A developing human is referred to as an embryo during weeks 3–8, and a fetus from the ninth week of gestation until birth," and notes that "By the end of the embryonic period, all of the organ systems are structured in rudimentary form," which is accomplished by organogenesis ("Within the first 8 weeks of gestation, a developing embryo establishes the rudimentary structures of all of its organs and tissues... This process is called organogenesis"). Thus the embryo-to-fetus relabeling at week 9 marks the point after rudimentary organs have been laid down. (Choice A) is correct: the fetal designation runs from the ninth week of gestation until birth, after organogenesis. (Choice B) is wrong because the first 2 weeks are the pre-embryonic stage and weeks 3–8 are the embryonic stage, so the term fetus does not apply from fertilization. (Choice C) confuses the start of the embryonic stage—gastrulation in the third week establishes the three germ layers—with the start of the fetal stage. (Choice D) names a real fetal milestone (quickening at weeks 16–20) but this occurs long after the embryo-to-fetus relabeling at week 9. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the textbook definition of the gestational period during which a developing human is termed a fetus.$ex21$, 'easy', '3B', $cc21$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc21$, 'biology', 1, 70
  ) RETURNING id
)
