-- Q22 [Gestation & Birth | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub22$Gestation & Birth$sub22$, $qt22$In a healthy near-term human fetus, deoxygenated blood is pumped from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. A physiologist predicts the fate of most of this blood given that the fetal lungs are fluid-filled and present a high resistance to flow. What happens to this right-ventricular output?$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "It crosses directly from the right atrium into the left atrium, bypassing the right ventricle entirely"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It is shunted from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, bypassing the high-resistance fetal lungs"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It is routed past the immature liver and delivered into the inferior vena cava"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It is carried from the aorta back into the pulmonary trunk to perfuse the developing lung tissue"}]$op22$::jsonb, 'B', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is B because the ductus arteriosus, a short muscular vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, diverts most of the right-ventricular output away from the nonfunctional fetal lungs and into the systemic aorta. The text states that "the ductus arteriosus is a short, muscular vessel that connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Most of the blood pumped from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk is thereby diverted into the aorta. Only enough blood reaches the fetal lungs to maintain the developing lung tissue." Because the uninflated fetal lungs have high resistance to flow, blood is encouraged toward the lower-resistance aorta, ensuring only a small volume passes through the immature pulmonary circuit. (Choice A) Crossing from the right atrium into the left atrium describes the foramen ovale, a separate interatrial shunt, not the ductus arteriosus, and this also misroutes blood around the right ventricle that has already entered the pulmonary trunk. (Choice B) is correct because the pulmonary-trunk-to-aorta diversion through the ductus arteriosus bypasses the high-resistance fetal lungs. (Choice C) Routing freshly oxygenated placental blood past the immature liver into the inferior vena cava describes the ductus venosus, the third fetal shunt branching from the umbilical vein. (Choice D) Aorta-to-pulmonary-trunk flow reverses the normal fetal direction and is in fact the pathology of a patent ductus arteriosus, in which blood flows from the higher-pressure aorta into the lower-pressure pulmonary trunk. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires using the high-resistance condition of the fluid-filled fetal lungs to predict the direction blood takes through a specific shunt.$ex22$, 'medium', '3B', $cc22$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
