-- Q23 [Gestation & Birth | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub23$Gestation & Birth$sub23$, $qt23$An obstetric physiologist monitoring a patient in active labor records that each wave of uterine contractions is followed by a measurable rise in circulating pituitary oxytocin and by progressively stronger subsequent contractions, right up until the newborn is delivered. Which mechanism best accounts for this self-amplifying escalation?$qt23$, $op23$[{"label": "A", "text": "Cervical stretch raises pituitary oxytocin secretion, and the added oxytocin relaxes the myometrium to restore its resting state, a negative feedback loop that ends at delivery"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Rising maternal progesterone progressively increases the myometrium's sensitivity to contractile stimuli, so each contraction recruits more muscle and grows stronger until delivery"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cervical stretch raises pituitary oxytocin secretion, and the added oxytocin intensifies the uterine contractions that stretch the cervix further, a positive feedback loop that ends at delivery"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Prostaglandins released first from the fetal membranes trigger the initial cervical stretch, which then recruits oxytocin to take over and drive the contractions until delivery"}]$op23$::jsonb, 'C', $ex23$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is C because true labor is driven by a positive feedback loop in which contraction-induced cervical stretch raises oxytocin secretion, and that oxytocin drives still stronger contractions. The text states that "uterine contractions stretch the cervix" and that "cervical dilation boosts oxytocin secretion from the pituitary, which in turn triggers more powerful uterine contractions," so each cycle amplifies rather than reverses the change, and "the feedback loop continues until birth." Because oxytocin strengthens contractions that further stretch the cervix and release still more oxytocin, the deviation is amplified, which is the defining feature of positive (not negative) feedback. (Choice A) is wrong because oxytocin "stimulates the contractions of labor" and increases their intensity rather than relaxing the myometrium, and labor is a positive, deviation-amplifying loop, not a deviation-correcting negative one. (Choice B) is wrong because it is the late-pregnancy drop in progesterone, paired with rising estrogen, that disinhibits the myometrium ("progesterone inhibits uterine contractions... progesterone levels plateau and then drop... because progesterone no longer inhibits them"); rising progesterone would suppress, not sensitize, contractions. (Choice C) is correct because it matches the described loop: cervical stretch increases oxytocin, which intensifies contractions that stretch the cervix further until birth. (Choice D) is wrong because the loop is driven by contraction-induced cervical stretch and oxytocin; prostaglandin secretion from the fetal membranes is downstream, stimulated when "oxytocin begins to stimulate stronger... uterine contractions, which—in a positive feedback loop—stimulate the secretion of prostaglandins," so prostaglandins amplify the loop rather than initiating the cervical stretch. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting an observed pattern of escalating contractions and oxytocin pulses and mapping it onto the deviation-amplifying logic of a positive feedback mechanism rather than recalling an isolated fact.$ex23$, 'medium', '3B', $cc23$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc23$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
