-- Q24 [Gestation & Birth | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub24$Gestation & Birth$sub24$, $qt24$In a near-term human fetus, a developmental biologist surgically obliterates the ductus venosus while leaving the umbilical vein, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus fully patent. She then asks what happens to the blood arriving from the placenta. Which outcome does she predict?$qt24$, $op24$[{"label": "A", "text": "Most deoxygenated blood entering the right atrium would be forced through the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk toward the lungs instead of crossing directly into the left atrium."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Most blood pumped from the right ventricle would be forced through the fluid-filled fetal lungs instead of being diverted into the aorta."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Most oxygen-depleted fetal blood would be forced to recirculate through the fetal body instead of returning to the placenta to unload its wastes."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Most freshly oxygenated placental blood would be forced through the immature fetal liver instead of passing largely directly into the inferior vena cava."}]$op24$::jsonb, 'D', $ex24$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is D because the ductus venosus is the shunt that branches from the umbilical vein and lets most of the freshly oxygenated placental blood bypass the fetal liver, routing it directly into the inferior vena cava; removing it forces that blood through the hepatic circulation instead. The text states the ductus venosus \"branches from the umbilical vein, allowing much of the freshly oxygenated blood from the placenta...to bypass the fetal liver and go directly to the fetal heart\" and is the \"shunt that causes oxygenated blood to bypass the fetal liver on its way to the inferior vena cava.\" The other two shunts named in the text divert blood from the pulmonary to the systemic circuit (foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus) and act on already-returned venous and right-heart blood, not on the incoming placental stream, so they cannot account for the placental blood's rerouting. Because only the ductus venosus connects the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava and bypasses the liver, its loss specifically forces placental blood through the liver. (Choice A) The right-atrium-to-left-atrium crossing that bypasses the lungs is the foramen ovale, not the ductus venosus, so obliterating the ductus venosus would not redirect right atrial blood to the right ventricle. (Choice B) Diverting right-ventricular output from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta is the role of the ductus arteriosus, which is left patent here, so right-ventricular blood would still bypass the lungs. (Choice C) Returning oxygen-depleted fetal blood and wastes to the placenta is the job of the umbilical arteries, a pathway untouched by ablation of the ductus venosus, so recirculation through the body would not result. (Choice D) Obliterating the ductus venosus removes the only liver-bypass for incoming placental blood, forcing it through the immature hepatic circulation rather than directly into the inferior vena cava. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the physiological consequence of removing one specific shunt by reasoning from each shunt's anatomical connections and the direction of blood flow, rather than recalling a single fact.$ex24$, 'hard', '3B', $cc24$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc24$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
