-- Q25 [Gestation & Birth | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Embryogenesis & Development', $sub25$Gestation & Birth$sub25$, $qt25$A full-term newborn takes its first breaths, yet an echocardiogram days later still shows blood passing directly between the two atria, and auscultation reveals a murmur. A cardiologist concludes that one fetal shunt failed to undergo its normal postnatal change. Which failure best accounts for this finding?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "The opening in the interatrial septum failed to seal, so a direct right-atrium-to-left-atrium communication persists instead of leaving only the fossa ovalis."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The vessel that branched from the umbilical vein failed to degenerate, so oxygenated blood keeps bypassing the liver instead of leaving only the ligamentum venosum."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The muscular channel between the pulmonary trunk and aorta failed to constrict, so right-ventricular output keeps bypassing the lungs instead of leaving only the ligamentum arteriosum."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The opening in the interatrial septum sealed on schedule, while the muscular vessel joining the pulmonary trunk and aorta degenerated normally into the ligamentum arteriosum."}]$op25$::jsonb, 'A', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Fetal Circulation & Birth'. The answer is A because a persistent direct opening between the right and left atria is the defining feature of a patent foramen ovale, which results when the interatrial septum fails to close after birth. The foramen ovale is "an opening in the interatrial septum that allows blood to flow from the right atrium to the left atrium," and as "the newborn begins to breathe and blood pressure in the atria increases, this shunt closes," normally leaving only the fossa ovalis. When closure fails, "the septum primum does not close at birth, and the fossa ovalis is unable to fuse," producing the persistent interatrial communication and murmur described in the scenario. The other shunts (ductus arteriosus, ductus venosus) connect different vessels and would not produce a direct atrium-to-atrium flow. (Choice A) Correct: failure of the interatrial septum to seal leaves a patent foramen ovale, a direct right-atrium-to-left-atrium opening rather than a fused fossa ovalis. (Choice B) Wrong shunt: persistence of the umbilical-vein-derived channel that bypasses the liver describes a failed ductus venosus (normally "degenerates to become the ligamentum venosum"), not an interatrial defect. (Choice C) Wrong shunt: a non-constricting pulmonary-trunk-to-aorta vessel is a patent ductus arteriosus (normally leaving "the ligamentum arteriosum"), which diverts ventricular output, not atrial blood between chambers. (Choice D) Describes normal closure of both structures, so it would produce neither the persistent interatrial flow nor the murmur observed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting a clinical scenario (persistent inter-atrial flow plus a murmur after the first breath) and reasoning back to which specific fetal shunt failed its normal postnatal closure.$ex25$, 'hard', '3B', $cc25$Fetal Circulation & Birth$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
